-- status  : Definition
-- author  : @Claude
-- created : 2026-09-05T04:28:27.528425+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/1bf8ee3c-9956-5a8c-870c-11962238c4d4
-- title:
--   Fibre product and tangent ring of a small extension
-- statement:
--   For a homomorphism $\sigma : T \to S$ of commutative rings, `fibreProd σ` is the subring $T \times_S T = \{(a,b) \in T \times T : \sigma a = \sigma b\}$ of $T \times T$, realised as the equaliser of the two composites of $\sigma$ with the product projections. It comes with the two ring maps `fst`, `snd` to $T$, the diagonal `diag : T →+* fibreProd σ`, the constructor `pair a b h` from a pair with $\sigma a = \sigma b$, an extensionality criterion in terms of the two coordinates, the observation that $\mathrm{snd}(x) - \mathrm{fst}(x) \in \ker\sigma$ for every $x$, and, for any base ring $A$ acting on $T$, the $A$-algebra structure obtained by composing the diagonal with $\mathrm{algebraMap}_{A,T}$.
--
--   With $T$ local, `diagMax σ` is the ideal of $T \times_S T$ generated by the diagonal image of the maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$, and the tangent ring `Tangent σ` is the quotient $(T \times_S T)/\mathrm{diagMax}$, with quotient map `mk` and residue map `res : Tangent σ →+* ResidueField T` induced by $\mathrm{fst}$ followed by the residue map; `res` is surjective. Smallness enters as the explicit hypothesis $\ker\sigma \cdot \mathfrak m = \bot$, under which $x \in \mathrm{diagMax}$ iff the two coordinates of $x$ agree and lie in $\mathfrak m$, and $\mathrm{mk}(x) = \mathrm{mk}(y)$ iff $\mathrm{fst}(x) - \mathrm{fst}(y) \in \mathfrak m$ and $\mathrm{snd}(x) - \mathrm{snd}(y) = \mathrm{fst}(x) - \mathrm{fst}(y)$. Under the separate hypothesis $\ker\sigma \le \mathfrak m$ (automatic when $S$ is nontrivial), $x$ is a unit iff $\mathrm{fst}(x)$ is, whence $T \times_S T$ and $\mathrm{Tangent}\,\sigma$ are local rings (stated as theorems, the locality instance being supplied explicitly where needed); the maximal ideal of the tangent ring is exactly $\ker(\mathrm{res})$, and with both hypotheses its square is zero. Two further results express that $x \mapsto (\mathrm{fst}(x), \mathrm{mk}(x))$ identifies $T \times_S T$ with the fibre product of $T$ and $\mathrm{Tangent}\,\sigma$ over the residue field: it is injective, and every pair $(a, z)$ with $\mathrm{residue}(a) = \mathrm{res}(z)$ is hit. Remaining items record when $\mathrm{mk}(\mathrm{diag}\,a)$ vanishes, the image of a natural number in the tangent ring, and, viewing $T \times_S T$ as a $T$-submodule of $T \times T$, that both $T \times_S T$ and $\mathrm{Tangent}\,\sigma$ are Artinian when $T$ is.
--
--   **Relation to Mathlib.** Mathlib supplies the ambient notions used here (`RingHom.eqLocus`, `IsLocalRing`, `ResidueField`, `IsArtinianRing`); the fibre product of a ring map with itself along with its tangent ring, in the form of a small extension, is the project's own construction.
--
--   **Where it is used.** The tangent ring converts the comparison of two lifts along a small extension into a single lift over a square-zero algebra over the residue field, and is used in the uniqueness half of a lifting criterion across small extensions in the deformation-theoretic part of the argument.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Definitions/Def_IsLocalRing_SmallExtensionTangent.lean

